  "user_scenario": "You are Lucia Esquivel, the mother of Mateo Esquivel, a junior at Calloway Creek High School who has been playing varsity football this fall. Mateo tore his ACL at practice in early September; his orthopedic doctor wrote a note that went to the school's athletic trainer a couple of days later, and he is done for the season. It has been a long fall of physical therapy, you sound a little worn out, and you keep things short and practical.\n\nYou are calling the Family Services Center today because you want to take Mateo off the football team officially and you want whatever credit the district gives back on his football fee. You paid the $85 fee online back in July and that is the only number you have in mind; you have no idea how the district works out a credit and you never suggest an amount. You start by saying who you are, that Mateo got hurt and is finished for the season, and that you'd like to withdraw him from football and find out what comes back on the fee.\n\nWhen the agent asks you to confirm your identity you give your phone number, 5125550163, and the last four digits of your driver's license, 4426. Only if they ask for something more do you add your date of birth, September 14, 1982, or your email, lucia.esquivel@outlook.com. Mateo's student ID number is 440426 and his birthday is February 11, 2010, if they need it. If the agent asks about his reason for leaving, it is the season-ending knee injury and nothing else; you'd like it done today. If the agent asks about school lunches, you remember filling out the free and reduced-price meal application at the end of July and getting a letter in August saying he was approved for reduced-price meals; you don't remember the exact dates and you'd rather the agent look them up.\n\nIf the agent tells you the account needs an adjustment of its own before the credit for leaving can be worked out, you say that's fine and to go ahead. When the agent explains that withdrawing Mateo is something you have to submit yourself and hands it over to you, you submit it right then, exactly with the details the agent gives you. If the agent reads you a credit figure before or after you submit, you just accept it; you don't argue with it and you don't try to check it against anything.\n\nOnce the agent tells you the withdrawal is recorded and what credit was booked on the fee, you thank them and end the call.",
